{"st":"IA","name":"Iowa","seats":[{"census":1840,"seats":2},{"census":1850,"seats":2},{"census":1860,"seats":6},{"census":1870,"seats":9},{"census":1880,"seats":11},{"census":1890,"seats":11},{"census":1900,"seats":11},{"census":1910,"seats":11},{"census":1920,"seats":11},{"census":1930,"seats":9},{"census":1940,"seats":8},{"census":1950,"seats":8},{"census":1960,"seats":7},{"census":1970,"seats":6},{"census":1980,"seats":6},{"census":1990,"seats":5},{"census":2000,"seats":5},{"census":2010,"seats":4},{"census":2020,"seats":4}],"events":[{"congress":29,"year":1844,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 29th Congress (1845–1847), Iowa elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":29,"year":1846,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Iowa became the 29th state on December 28, 1846. Congress gave it 2 seats in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Iowa kept 2 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Iowa's House delegation grew from 2 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Iowa's House delegation grew from 6 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Iowa's House delegation grew from 9 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Iowa kept 11 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Iowa kept 11 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Iowa kept 11 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Iowa's House delegation shrank from 11 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Iowa's House delegation shrank from 9 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Iowa kept 8 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Iowa's House delegation shrank from 8 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Iowa's House delegation shrank from 7 to 6 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":97,"year":1981,"kind":"other","title":"Nonpartisan staff draw the maps","text":"Since 1980, nonpartisan legislative staff (now the Legislative Services Agency) have drafted Iowa's congressional maps without using party data or incumbent addresses. The legislature may only accept or reject the first two plans and may amend a third.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/iowa/"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Iowa kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Iowa's House delegation shrank from 6 to 5 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Iowa kept 5 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2011,"kind":"other","title":"First staff plan approved for four seats","text":"With Iowa dropping from five seats to four, the legislature approved the first congressional plan drafted by the Legislative Services Agency on April 18, 2011. The governor signed it April 29.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/iowa/"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Iowa's House delegation shrank from 5 to 4 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2021,"kind":"other","title":"Second staff plan approved","text":"The legislature rejected the Legislative Services Agency's first proposed plan on October 5, 2021, then approved its second proposal on October 28. The governor signed it November 4.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/iowa/"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Iowa kept 4 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]}],"compactness":{"c029":{"IA-0":0.572},"c030":{"IA-1":0.424,"IA-2":0.434},"c031":{"IA-1":0.437,"IA-2":0.427},"c032":{"IA-1":0.437,"IA-2":0.427},"c033":{"IA-1":0.437,"IA-2":0.427},"c034":{"IA-1":0.437,"IA-2":0.427},"c035":{"IA-1":0.437,"IA-2":0.427},"c036":{"IA-1":0.456,"IA-2":0.496},"c037":{"IA-1":0.456,"IA-2":0.496},"c038":{"IA-1":0.466,"IA-2":0.591,"IA-3":0.511,"IA-4":0.432,"IA-5":0.589,"IA-6